<header><p class="enc-kicker">Place · region</p><h1 class="enc-title">KwaZulu-Natal</h1></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">KwaZulu-Natal is a province on the east coast of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/south-africa">South Africa</a>, created in 1994 by merging the province of Natal with the KwaZulu homeland. Its largest city is <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/durban">Durban</a> and its capital <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/pietermaritzburg">Pietermaritzburg</a>. The region was the heartland of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/zulu-kingdom">Zulu Kingdom</a>, became the British colony of Natal in the 19th century, and remains home to most of South Africa's Zulu-speaking population.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/kwazulu-natal.jpg" alt="KwaZulu-Natal" width="320" height="171" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: South African Tourism from South Africa · CC BY 2.0 · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:South_Beach,_Durban,_KwaZulu-Natal,_South_Africa_(20504187132).jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
<section class="enc-more"><h2 class="enc-more-h">Setting</h2><p>KwaZulu-Natal lies in the south-east of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/south-africa">South Africa</a>, with a long shoreline on the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/indian-ocean">Indian Ocean</a>, and covers about 92,100 square kilometres, roughly the size of Portugal. It has three geographic areas: a lowland coast that is very narrow in the south and wider in the north, the undulating plateau of the Natal Midlands, and two mountain areas, the western <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/drakensberg">Drakensberg</a>, a basalt wall rising over 3,000 metres beside <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/lesotho">Lesotho</a>, and the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/lubombo-mountains">Lebombo Mountains</a> in the north. The Tugela, the largest river, flows across the centre of the province.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">History</h2><p>On Christmas Day 1497 the Portuguese explorer <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/vasco-da-gama">Vasco da Gama</a> saw the coast and named it Natal, the Portuguese word for Christmas. The <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/nguni">Nguni</a> branch of the Bantu had occupied the area from the early 1300s. The first European settlers, mostly British, established <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/durban">Port Natal</a> as a trading post and made almost no attempt to develop the interior, whose inhabitants had been decimated by the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/zulu-kingdom">Zulu</a> king Shaka. Afrikaner <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/voortrekkers">Voortrekkers</a> entered through the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/drakensberg">Drakensberg</a> passes in 1837, defeated the Zulus at the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/battle-of-blood-river">Battle of Blood River</a> in 1838 and established the Republic of Natal, which lasted from 1839 until <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/britain">Britain</a> annexed it in 1843.</p><p>From 1860 the British brought increasing numbers of Indians, mainly <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/tamils">Tamils</a>, to work the coastal <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/sugar-plantation">sugar</a> plantations. The colony acquired Zululand, north of the Tugela, after the Zulu War of 1879, and the lands north of the Buffalo River in 1902. In the South African War of 1899 to 1902 <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/boers">Boer</a> forces besieged Ladysmith, and for three months the line between the two armies followed the Tugela. The colony became a province of the Union of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/south-africa">South Africa</a> in 1910.</p><p>In 1994, after the end of apartheid, the KwaZulu homeland, whose name means Place of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/zulu-kingdom">Zulu</a>, was reincorporated into Natal and the province was renamed KwaZulu-Natal. It is the only South African province with the name of its dominant ethnic group in its name.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">The place today</h2><p><a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/pietermaritzburg">Pietermaritzburg</a> is the seat of the provincial government and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/durban">Durban</a> the largest city, which has the busiest port in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/africa">Africa</a> by most measures. At the 2022 <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/census">census</a> the province had 12,423,907 inhabitants, the second-most populous in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/south-africa">South Africa</a>, and about 82 percent gave <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/zulu-kingdom">Zulu</a> as their first language. It is home to the Zulu monarch, whose constitutional powers are limited. The iSimangaliso Wetland Park and the uKhahlamba Drakensberg Park are <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/unesco-world-heritage-site">UNESCO World Heritage Sites</a>, and Hluhluwe–Imfolozi Park is where the southern white rhinoceros was saved from extinction.</p><p class="enc-more-src">This account follows the Wikipedia article “KwaZulu-Natal” (CC BY-SA 4.0), linked under Read further below.</p></section>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote>At the start of the nineteenth century the Zulu were a small Nguni-speaking chiefdom on the White Mfolozi river, one of many in the land between the Phongolo and Thukela rivers in what is now <b>KwaZulu-Natal</b>.</blockquote><a href="/empire/zulu-kingdom">Zulu Kingdom · Foundation</a></li><li><blockquote>Tools almost identical to those of modern San, found at Border Cave in <b>KwaZulu-Natal</b>, date to 42,000 BCE.</blockquote><a href="/culture/san-people">San people · Origins</a></li><li><blockquote>The Hlubi were an Nguni chiefdom settled near the upper Mzinyathi (Buffalo) river in what is now northern <b>KwaZulu-Natal</b>.</blockquote><a href="/state/hlubi-chiefdom">Hlubi Chiefdom</a></li><li><blockquote>Merged with the KwaZulu homeland in 1994, it is now the province of <b>KwaZulu-Natal</b>.</blockquote><a href="/state/natal-colony">Natal Colony</a></li><li><blockquote>The Ndwandwe were settled around the Mkhuze and Pongola rivers in the north of present-day <b>KwaZulu-Natal</b>, with ties to the Dlamini of the Swazi Kingdom.</blockquote><a href="/state/ndwandwe-kingdom">Ndwandwe Kingdom</a></li><li><blockquote>By the 18th century the Qwabe were settled near the lower Mhlathuze river in what is now <b>KwaZulu-Natal</b>.</blockquote><a href="/state/qwabe-chiefdom">Qwabe Chiefdom</a></li></ul></section>
